# Answer 1 · The Pros and Cons of Gender Quotas and Their Impact in Pakistan (CSS 2016)
@py question 2016-4

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Define quotas and their types, weigh the pros against the cons, then judge Pakistan's experience — what quotas have achieved and where they fall short — and end with reforms.
@ask ① What are the PROS and CONS of GENDER QUOTA in politics?
@ask ② Your VIEWS on the IMPACT of gender quota in politics in PAKISTAN
(1) Introduction: quotas are a fast track to women's presence, but presence is not yet power
(2) What a quota is: CEDAW Article 4(1); Pakistan's Article 25(3)
(3) Types: reserved seats, candidate quotas, party quotas
(4) Pro 1: a fast track over generations of slow progress
(5) Pro 2: corrects barriers and gives descriptive representation
(6) Pro 3: better policy, role models and a critical mass
(7) Con 1: the merit objection and tokenism
(8) Con 2: proxies, stigma and elite capture
(9) Con 3: a ceiling not a floor; root causes untouched
(10) Impact in Pakistan — gains: numbers, laws, caucus, local councillors
(11) Impact in Pakistan — limits: party dependence, few general seats, no ministers
(12) My view, reforms and conclusion
:::

## Decoding the question
Two parts. **"Pros and cons"** asks for a **balanced** treatment of arguments for and against quotas in general. **"Give your views on the impact… in Pakistan"** asks for a **judgement** based on Pakistani evidence — numbers, laws and limits. Use the theory of **representation** (descriptive vs substantive) to frame your view, and end with reforms.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
A **gender quota** is a rule that requires a **minimum number or share of women** in a political body — a legislature, a candidate list or a party organ.

#### ii) The argument
Quotas are the fastest proven way to bring women into politics, and their **benefits outweigh their costs**. In Pakistan the quota has delivered **presence** — about one-fifth of the National Assembly — and real **laws for women**, but not yet **power**, because reserved-seat women depend on party leaders. Better design, not abolition, is the answer.

### 2. What a quota is
Quotas are **temporary special measures**. **Article 4(1)** of **CEDAW** (==1979==) declares that such measures "shall not be considered discrimination", and Pakistan's **Article 25(3)** allows special provision for women. Their aim is to correct a historical imbalance until equal participation becomes normal. More than **130** countries now use some form of political gender quota, and women's share of seats in national parliaments worldwide has risen to a little over a quarter — much of that rise driven by quotas.

### 3. Types of quota
1. **Reserved seats** guarantee a result: Pakistan's ==60== National Assembly seats, Bangladesh's ==50==.
2. **Legislated candidate quotas** require parties to nominate women: Pakistan's **5%** of general tickets (**Elections Act 2017**), France's parity law (==2000==).
3. **Voluntary party quotas** are parties' own rules, common in Scandinavia.

### 4. Pro 1: a fast track
Without quotas, women's representation rises very slowly. The Danish scholar **Drude Dahlerup** contrasted the Nordic "**incremental track**" of many decades with the "**fast track**" of quotas. Pakistan proves the point: in the ==1990s==, when reserved seats lapsed, only **two to six** women sat in a National Assembly of over 200; in ==2002==, with 60 seats, women passed **20%** at once.

### 5. Pro 2: correcting barriers and representing women

#### i) Barriers, not lack of merit
Women face **party gatekeeping**, costly campaigns, norms of purdah and honour, and violence. A quota corrects an **unequal starting line**. Men already benefit from an unwritten "quota" of family networks, land and money; a gender quota simply opens the gate to women as well.

#### ii) Descriptive representation
**Hanna Pitkin** (==1967==) called representatives who resemble the people **descriptive** representatives. A parliament that is 95% male cannot claim to reflect a society that is half female.

### 6. Pro 3: policy, role models and critical mass

#### i) Better policy
In India, **Chattopadhyay and Duflo** (==2004==) found that women heads of village councils, chosen through quotas, invested more in **drinking water** — a priority for women.

#### ii) Role models
Seeing women in office raises girls' aspirations and reduces voters' bias.

#### iii) Critical mass
Above about **30%**, women stop being isolated **tokens** (Kanter, ==1977==) and can form alliances across parties, as Pakistan's caucus later did.

#### iv) Comparative evidence
Elsewhere in South Asia the pattern holds. **Nepal**'s constitutional one-third quota, filled largely through proportional-representation lists, produced about **35%** women in its House in ==2026==, while **Sri Lanka**, without a national quota, reached a record of only **22** women in a house of 225 in ==2024==. Quotas, not culture, explain the difference; Pakistan, with reserved seats, sits between the two at about one-fifth of its National Assembly. Design choices explain the gaps between these three neighbours.

### 7. Con 1: merit and tokenism
Critics argue that quotas promote people for their sex rather than ability, and produce **tokens** who are visible but powerless. The reply is that "merit" in Pakistani politics is already distorted by money, land and *biradari*; many reserved-seat women are highly educated professionals; and studies of Sweden found that quotas pushed out **mediocre men** rather than lowering quality.

### 8. Con 2: proxies, stigma and elite capture

#### i) Proxies
Seats may go to the wives, daughters and sisters of party leaders — "*biwi-beti-behen*" politics.

#### ii) Stigma
Quota members may be dismissed as "nominated" rather than elected.

#### iii) Elite capture
Seats tend to go to urban, educated or dynastic women, not to the rural poor.

### 9. Con 3: a ceiling, and root causes untouched
Parties may treat the quota as women's **maximum** share and give them few general tickets. Quotas also do not by themselves remove the root causes of exclusion — norms, money and violence. A woman may sit in parliament while women in her own district still cannot vote freely, own land or travel safely; critics therefore call quotas a **cosmetic** remedy if nothing else changes.

::: fig compare | The case for and against gender quotas.
## Pros
- Fast track to equal participation
- Corrects barriers, not merit
- Descriptive representation and justice
- Policy attention to women's needs
- Role models and critical mass
## Cons
- Merit objection; tokenism
- Proxies for male relatives
- Stigma of "nominated" seats
- Elite capture; ceiling not floor
- Root causes left untouched
:::

### 10. Impact in Pakistan — the gains

#### i) Numbers
Since the **Legal Framework Order 2002**, women hold **60** National Assembly seats, **17** in the Senate and **128** (now 132) in the provinces; under the **Devolution Plan** (==2000–01==) about **36,000** women became councillors.

#### ii) Laws
Women legislators, organised in the cross-party **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** (==2008==), drove the **workplace harassment law** (==2010==), the **acid** and **anti-women practices** laws (==2011==), the **NCSW Act** (==2012==) and the **honour-killing** and **anti-rape** laws (==2016==).

#### iii) Agenda
Domestic violence, harassment and inheritance became normal business of the assemblies; monitoring groups such as **FAFEN** found women members among the most active in moving questions, resolutions and private members' bills.

### 11. Impact in Pakistan — the limits
Reserved seats are filled from **party lists**, so members owe their place to leaders and rarely defy them. Only **11** women won general seats in ==2024==, and parties give women few winnable tickets despite the 5% rule. In ==2025== there were **no women federal ministers**. Reserved-seat women lack constituencies and development funds, and local quotas have been cut since 2013. Many reserved seats have gone to relatives of party leaders, confirming the proxy criticism, and the stigma of being "nominated" persists in debates on the floor of the House.

### 12. My view, reforms and conclusion

#### i) My view
The quota has achieved **descriptive** and partly **substantive** representation; its weakness lies in its **design**, not its principle. The lesson of the 1990s — when the quota lapsed and women almost vanished from parliament — is that Pakistan cannot yet do without it.

#### ii) Reforms
Move towards **direct election** of women's seats; raise the candidate quota and require **winnable** seats; impose sanctions; give all members equal resources; restore **33%** local quotas.

#### iii) Conclusion
Quotas have real costs, but the alternative — near-exclusion, as in the 1990s — is worse. Pakistan's quota has brought women into the assemblies and produced landmark laws. The next task is to turn presence into **power**.

## Why this answer scores
- Gives **both sides** before judging, as "pros and cons" requires.
- Uses **theory** (Dahlerup, Pitkin, Kanter) and **evidence** (India, Sweden, Pakistan's 1990s).
- Gives a clear **personal view** on Pakistan, backed by laws and data.
- A **compare figure** sets out the debate at a glance.
